ller now faces a serious challenge from Democrat Brian Poindexter amid domestic violence allegations that have drawn national coverage. Miller still holds a clear money advantage, with over $2.1 million raised and $1.4 million on hand, and the district carries a five-point Republican lean. The NRCC has lined up new ad spending to defend him, and both sides are trading attack ads on local television.\n\nPolling from sponsors on both sides shows Poindexter ahead by margins ranging from five to fourteen points, though the surveys are from early August. Nearly every professional rating calls the race a tossup, with Split Ticket the lone outlier at Lean D, and prediction markets price Poindexter around 78%. Affordability has emerged as a central campaign theme in a contest that could help decide House control.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6543, "margin": 2.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7358, "margin": 3.5}]}